# config/Kconfig.audio — The audio server's policy
#
# The interface (crates/core/devif/src/sound.rs, `Sound`) fixes what a stream
# may name: its bounds are constants of that file. The options here are what a
# build lets the audio server (AUDIOD.ELF, userspace/services/audiod) accept
# inside them, so every range
# stops at the interface's bound (tests/host/devif-tests/tests/sound.rs keeps
# the two equal). The devices themselves are `[audio.NAME]` rows of the signed
# topology (TOPOLOGY_MAX_AUDIO in config/Kconfig.security); which hardware
# serves a row is the driver tables' business, not this menu's.

menu "Audio"

config AUDIOD
    bool "Audio server (AUDIOD.ELF): mixes the clients' streams into the one device stream"
    depends on DRV_VIRTIO_SND
    default y if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED && !USE_ROBOT && !USE_INDUSTRIAL
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries a row for AUDIOD.ELF and the kernel starts
      it at boot. The server serves the `Sound` interface on `endpoint.audio`
      (userspace/services/audiod): a client opens a stream, hands it periods of
      memory it made, and the server mixes the streams (a saturating sum, a
      per-stream gain, the device's rate and format, one stream's underrun its
      own) into the one playback stream it holds on the kernel's `sound.0`.
      Capture is not mixed: the client's own objects are handed to the kernel's
      microphone gate, which needs the `mic` effect of the CLIENT's class (the
      server holds none) and records every attempt that reaches it, naming the
      client (AUDIO_CAPTURE). `toolbox beep` goes through the server when its row
      holds `endpoint.audio` and straight to `sound.0` otherwise; with the
      server, the toolbox row holds no `sound.0` (the device has one holder).

      The row is a deadline reservation of 1 ms every 10 ms (10 % of a CPU,
      `hard_rt`, priority 12) that keeps the device fed under load. A robot or
      industrial image has no speaker to share and is n; a small board is n
      (native drivers only: it has no virtio-sound).

      Cost when set: one task of AUDIOD_MEM_PAGES pages (the topology
      builder), two threads (16 KiB stack for the pump), 48 KiB of static
      state, and 3 + 3 memory objects open at a time per stream.


config AUDIO_CAPTURE
    bool "Audio server: capture streams (the microphone)"
    default y if !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default n
    help
      y: the audio server opens `DIR_CAPTURE` streams. The kernel's gate
      admits each one on the `mic` effect of the REQUESTER's scheduler class
      (an `[envelope.NAME]` row with `kind = "mic"`): the client that made the
      call the server is serving, which the server names to the kernel by the
      call's warrant (`Sound::open`'s `on_behalf`). The server's own class is
      never consulted and the `audio-capture` decision record names the
      client, so holding `endpoint.audio` is not holding the microphone: only
      a client whose class has the effect listens, and one without it is
      refused with a durable record naming it. A task that calls `sound.0`
      itself is its own requester.
      n: the capture path is not compiled; a capture `open` answers
      UNSUPPORTED and no envelope row can enable it. An image that must never
      listen is built with n, which no topology can undo.

      Small boards name it: a HWCLASS_EMBEDDED build is n unless the config
      says y.

      Cost: no static RAM. The code of the capture path, not measured.

config AUDIO_MAX_STREAMS
    int "Audio server: streams open at once"
    range 1 32
    default 2 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      Streams (open `Sound` capabilities, playback and capture together) the
      audio server holds. The next `open` answers BUSY. Each stream is one
      slot of the server's static table; a device that mixes several
      clients' streams into one hardware stream uses one slot per client.

      Cost: one table slot per stream (AUDIOD's stream record, 160 bytes),
      not measured on a board.

config AUDIO_MAX_PERIODS
    int "Audio server: most periods in one stream's arena"
    range 2 64
    default 8 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      The most periods a client may name in `Params::periods`; more is
      BAD_ARGUMENT. The interface's own bound is 64 (`PERIODS_MAX`, one bit a
      period in `Posted`). Latency is `periods * period_frames / rate`: a
      stream that wants a small delay uses few short periods, and the bound
      keeps one client from holding the server's queues for seconds.

      Cost: none by itself; each period is client memory (the arena), and the
      two queues of a stream are `SpscSlots<4>` of the next power of two above
      the periods (one slot is the drain end marker; 256 B header + 32 B a slot, in the client's memory
      objects).

config AUDIO_ARENA_KB_MAX
    int "Audio server: largest arena of one stream (KiB)"
    range 4 16384
    default 64 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4096
    help
      The most memory a client may hand the server as one stream's arena
      (`periods * period_frames * channels * sample bytes`); a bigger geometry
      is BAD_ARGUMENT. The interface's own bound is 16 MiB
      (`ARENA_BYTES_MAX`). The arena is the client's memory, mapped by the
      server for the stream's life, so the bound caps what a client can pin
      in the server's address space, not what the kernel allocates.

      Cost: no static RAM.

endmenu
